 "note": "CSymbol64 -> authored bone name. A hash IS symbol64(name), so every entry is a verified preimage rather than a label invented here. THE ALGEBRA: symbol64 is r = (r<<8) ^ SEEDS[r>>56] ^ b seeded with 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (NOT 0 -- reimplementing it from 0 silently breaks every delta), and SEEDS is GF(2)-linear, so with T(x) = (x<<8)^SEEDS[x>>56] the hash is affine: H(a+b) == T^|b|(H(a)) ^ fold0(b). Three consequences carry the whole attack. (1) LENGTH ORACLE. A one-character difference changes the hash by exactly T^d(charA^charB), depending only on the character XOR and its distance from the END. XOR two L/R siblings, decode the delta as T^d('l'^'r'), and n = d+5 because the side letter sits at index 4. That pinned the EXACT length of 66 of the 74 unknown chassis hashes (21,23,24,25,26,27) before a single candidate was hashed, and decoding the intra-group deltas pinned the varying DIGIT POSITIONS too -- index 17 taking 3 values, index 18 taking 4 -- which is how Leg1Piston<1-3> and SpinePiston<1-4> were read off the hashes as a shape, then confirmed as names. (2) FREE 7-CHARACTER TAIL. While x < 2^56 the seed term of T is zero, so T is a plain byte shift and fold0 of any fragment of at most 7 bytes is just those bytes packed big-endian. Hence symbol64(head+tail7) == T^7(symbol64(head)) ^ int(tail7): the LAST SEVEN CHARACTERS fall out by XOR and never have to be guessed. Combined with a pinned length n, only the middle needs enumerating -- n = 7 (prefix) + L (middle) + 7 (tail), L = n-14 -- and fixing L exactly shrinks a token walk from 1e13 sequences to thousands. ThrusterUlnar1 fell out of a 7-character middle that is not even a whole word ('thruste', a cut of 'thruster'), so let the final token be CUT by the tail boundary; a free-character hack cannot reach a word straddling it. (3) COLLISION BUDGET. The free tail accepts ANY printable filling, so a head space H explores ~H * 37^7 names and yields ~H * 37^7 * targets / 2^64 FALSE preimages -- all verifying against symbol64, all meaningless. ~1.6e8 heads is about ten of them at a dozen targets; a 2.3e11 head space produced ~14000 and buried the real names. Size the stem list to the budget, print it before running, and sift survivors by word-segmentation (nltk 'words' plus the authored vocabulary): a real name is a few long tokens, a collision needs many short scraps. PITFALL: index any final-token table by VALUE to a LIST, not a scalar. Sibling names sharing a prefix collide EXACTLY by construction -- tg_inner ^ f('inner1') == tg_outer ^ f('outer1') -- so a dict silently drops one of every pair; that hid BrowInner/CheekInner behind BrowOuter/CheekOuter. Do NOT try to invert the step: SEEDS[h]&0xFF is not injective. SOURCES, in order of yield: quest_combat_port's hash dictionary; EchoVR-Cosmetics-Editor's Data/symbols.json (8064 verified pairs, and a 3241-word authored vocabulary once split on separators and CamelCase -- mine it before generating anything); generation over the naming grammar EXP_<side><n>_<part><n>[<mod><n>]; and the affine attack above. Bone-name strings DO ship in Lone Echo 2: loneecho2.exe carries 139 literal EXP_ names (that is where ArmGameIkHand1 came from), and echovr.exe/loneecho.exe carry ~135 more, but they are all the shared body rig -- 0 of the chassis-specific hashes appear in any binary, because those bones are authored per cosmetic and only ever stored as hashes.",
